WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.513
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.813 --> 00:00:08.851
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:17.364
Unit twenty-six: Language Endangerment. In this unit, you will discuss why languages disappear and how to protect them.

4
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:19.874
endangered language.

5
00:00:20.574 --> 00:00:24.814
endangered language means a language at risk of disappearing.

6
00:00:25.114 --> 00:00:28.123
Nearly half of the world's languages are endangered.

7
00:00:28.823 --> 00:00:32.277
Your turn. Say: endangered language.

8
00:00:34.977 --> 00:00:36.570
endangered language.

9
00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:39.467
language revitalisation.

10
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:45.301
language revitalisation means efforts to bring a language back into everyday use.

11
00:00:45.601 --> 00:00:48.901
Hawaiian is a model of language revitalisation.

12
00:00:49.601 --> 00:00:53.507
Your turn. Say: language revitalisation.

13
00:00:56.207 --> 00:00:58.021
language revitalisation.

14
00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:00.668
linguistic diversity.

15
00:01:01.368 --> 00:01:06.363
linguistic diversity means the variety of languages in a region or the world.

16
00:01:06.663 --> 00:01:10.090
Papua New Guinea has extraordinary linguistic diversity.

17
00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.128
Your turn. Say: linguistic diversity.

18
00:01:16.828 --> 00:01:18.456
linguistic diversity.

19
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.753
last speaker.

20
00:01:21.453 --> 00:01:25.055
last speaker means the final fluent speaker of a language.

21
00:01:25.355 --> 00:01:28.759
When the last speaker dies, the language is lost.

22
00:01:29.459 --> 00:01:32.820
Your turn. Say: last speaker.

23
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:36.637
last speaker.

24
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:38.883
oral tradition.

25
00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:43.905
oral tradition means stories and knowledge passed down by speaking.

26
00:01:44.205 --> 00:01:48.492
Many indigenous histories survive only through oral tradition.

27
00:01:49.192 --> 00:01:52.239
Your turn. Say: oral tradition.

28
00:01:54.939 --> 00:01:56.079
oral tradition.

29
00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:58.584
language policy.

30
00:01:59.284 --> 00:02:04.894
language policy means government decisions about which languages are used and taught.

31
00:02:05.194 --> 00:02:09.562
Language policy shapes education in multilingual countries.

32
00:02:10.262 --> 00:02:13.483
Your turn. Say: language policy.

33
00:02:16.183 --> 00:02:17.567
language policy.

34
00:02:18.583 --> 00:02:20.037
minority language.

35
00:02:20.737 --> 00:02:25.291
minority language means a language spoken by a small group in a country.

36
00:02:25.591 --> 00:02:28.392
Bunong is a minority language in Mondulkiri.

37
00:02:29.092 --> 00:02:32.394
Your turn. Say: minority language.

38
00:02:35.094 --> 00:02:36.443
minority language.

39
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.101
mother-tongue education.

40
00:02:39.801 --> 00:02:44.053
mother-tongue education means teaching children in their first language.

41
00:02:44.353 --> 00:02:48.048
Mother-tongue education improves early literacy.

42
00:02:48.748 --> 00:02:52.805
Your turn. Say: mother-tongue education.

43
00:02:55.505 --> 00:02:57.226
mother-tongue education.

44
00:02:58.217 --> 00:03:00.402
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:00.702 --> 00:03:06.510
Ms. Sarah, I heard that some indigenous languages in northeastern Cambodia are endangered.

46
00:03:07.817 --> 00:03:14.033
That's right. Many are being replaced by national languages as young people move to cities.

47
00:03:15.317 --> 00:03:16.887
Is anything being done?

48
00:03:18.183 --> 00:03:22.624
Yes. Bilingual programmes have been introduced in several provinces.

49
00:03:23.917 --> 00:03:26.613
So children are being taught in their mother tongue first?

50
00:03:27.917 --> 00:03:33.518
Exactly. Mother-tongue education is known to improve learning outcomes.

51
00:03:34.817 --> 00:03:39.196
And when the last speaker dies, a whole oral tradition is lost.

52
00:03:40.483 --> 00:03:46.755
Which is why linguistic diversity needs to be protected, just like biodiversity.

53
00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:51.594
Grammar focus: Advanced passives (extended).

54
00:03:51.894 --> 00:03:55.681
Varied passive forms keep academic writing objective.

55
00:03:55.981 --> 00:03:59.969
Languages are being replaced. Programmes have been introduced.

56
00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:03.336
Mother-tongue education is known to improve learning.

57
00:04:03.636 --> 00:04:09.978
Varied passives keep academic writing objective and focused on the issue rather than the actors.

58
00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:14.831
Now read and listen. Every Language Is a Library.

59
00:04:15.131 --> 00:04:18.884
Around seven thousand languages are spoken in the world today.

60
00:04:20.183 --> 00:04:25.016
Linguists estimate that almost half of them could disappear by the end of this century.

61
00:04:26.317 --> 00:04:30.371
Many endangered languages are spoken only by elderly people.

62
00:04:31.683 --> 00:04:39.349
When the last speaker dies, centuries of oral tradition, medicine and ecological knowledge can be lost.

63
00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:45.935
In Cambodia, several indigenous languages are spoken in the northeastern provinces.

64
00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:52.953
Bilingual mother-tongue education programmes have been introduced to support these communities.

65
00:04:54.250 --> 00:05:01.033
Language revitalisation has also been successful in places such as Hawaii and New Zealand.

66
00:05:02.317 --> 00:05:07.312
Protecting linguistic diversity is increasingly seen as protecting human heritage itself.

67
00:05:08.617 --> 00:05:11.257
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:11.557 --> 00:05:14.695
Efforts to bring a language back into use are blank.

69
00:05:14.995 --> 00:05:21.226
A, language revitalisation. B, oral tradition. C, language policy.

70
00:05:25.217 --> 00:05:28.424
The answer is A: language revitalisation.

71
00:05:29.917 --> 00:05:32.021
The old school blank at the moment.

72
00:05:32.321 --> 00:05:37.936
A, is renovated. B, is being renovated. C, renovating.

73
00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:45.122
The answer is B: is being renovated.

74
00:05:46.617 --> 00:05:48.895
Reading blank to improve vocabulary.

75
00:05:49.195 --> 00:05:52.941
A, is known. B, knows. C, has known.

76
00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:58.857
The answer is A: is known.

77
00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:03.139
Teaching children in their first language is blank.

78
00:06:03.439 --> 00:06:09.240
A, mother-tongue education. B, minority language. C, linguistic diversity.

79
00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:16.712
The answer is A: mother-tongue education.

80
00:06:18.217 --> 00:06:23.165
According to the text, how many languages could disappear by the end of the century?

81
00:06:23.465 --> 00:06:28.430
A, about ten percent. B, almost half. C, all of them.

82
00:06:32.417 --> 00:06:34.881
The answer is B: almost half.

83
00:06:36.383 --> 00:06:41.567
Now it's your turn. Part one: Why should we protect minority languages?

84
00:06:46.867 --> 00:06:52.558
Part two: Give two reasons using passive forms, and suggest one practical step.

85
00:06:58.858 --> 00:07:10.915
Minority languages should be protected because they carry unique knowledge, and identity is closely linked to language. One step would be that more teachers are trained to teach in children's first language.

86
00:07:11.215 --> 00:07:14.015
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:15.017 --> 00:07:20.165
Great work today! You learned eight new words and advanced passives (extended).

88
00:07:20.465 --> 00:07:24.778
Today's value is respect for culture. Be proud of our Khmer culture.

89
00:07:25.078 --> 00:07:28.392
See you in Unit twenty-seven. Goodbye!

90
00:07:28.692 --> 00:07:31.158
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

